<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Muslim, Muslims, Islamic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Islam is a monotheistic religion founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the seventh century CE through the teaching of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. It shaped the founding and governance of many empires, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a>, Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> to the Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires. It also spread through trade and conquest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, where it shaped states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islam.jpg" alt="Islam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Melina/Agência Brasil · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His capital, Bandar Aceh Darussalam near the mouth of the Aceh River, later called Kutaraja by the Dutch and now Banda Aceh, became the seat of a dynasty that identified itself firmly with <b>Islam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aghlabids carried <b>Islam</b> across the central Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The state probably took shape from the thirteenth century onwards, as <b>Islam</b> spread inland from the coastal towns and as pastoral clans competed for scarce water and grazing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of <b>Islam</b> and the Arab conquests of Arabia and Egypt in the seventh century completed Aksum's commercial isolation, ending the trade-based prosperity that had sustained the kingdom for some five centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1307 Bularghu, the Mongol representative in Cilicia and a recent convert to <b>Islam</b>, murdered King Levon III, the former king Het'um II and their party; Oshin drove him out and the Ilkhan Oljeitu executed him, but the kingdom had lost its ally.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Shafi'i jurisprudence predominated at the Ayyubid court, though the dynasty also endowed institutions for the other Sunni schools, and the sultans cultivated the religious legitimacy essential to their role as defenders of <b>Islam</b> and protectors of the holy places.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
